"A profile diagram defines a controlled UML vocabulary through stereotypes, metaclass extensions, properties, and constraints.\n\nUse when: Standardize domain-specific annotations across UML models. Explain which modeling elements may carry custom metadata.\n\nAvoid when: Simple labels are sufficient and no metamodel extension is needed.\n\nVisual grammar: Stereotype boxes identify domain-specific modeling concepts. Extension notation connects stereotypes to eligible UML metaclasses. Properties and constraints define additional metadata and valid combinations.\n\nBuild: State the modeling problem that requires an extension. Identify the smallest set of metaclasses to extend. Define stereotype properties and constraints with precise names. Separate stereotype inheritance from metaclass extensions using clear routing and a notation key.\n\nCheck: Every stereotype extends a valid metaclass or inherits a justified extension. Property types and required values are stated. The profile adds semantics rather than renaming ordinary elements. Extension arrowheads and constraint text remain visibly separated.\n\nPitfalls: Using stereotypes as decorative status badges without definitions. Confusing an application domain class with a UML metaclass.\n\nExample: A Service stereotype extends Component and adds an owner property plus a required contract identifier.\n\nAlternatives: Class diagram, Component diagram."
